-- title:
--   Every K-differential of F is g dt when ordᵥ t ≠ 0
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ and $F$ be fields with $F$ a $K$-algebra, and assume $K$ has characteristic zero. Let $x \in F$ be such that $F$ is algebraic over the intermediate field $K(x) =$ `IntermediateField.adjoin K {x}` generated by $x$ (an instance hypothesis on $x$). Let $v$ be a place of $F$ over $K$ in the sense of the structure [`AlgebraicCurve.Place`](def/AlgebraicCurve_DivisorClassGroup.html#L22): a valuation subring of $F$ which contains $\operatorname{algebraMap} K F(a)$ for every $a \in K$, is not all of $F$, and is a principal ideal ring. Let $t \in F$ satisfy $v.\mathrm{ord}\, t \neq 0$, where $v.\mathrm{ord}$ is the integer $-\log$ of the value of $t$ under the $\mathbb{Z}^{m0}$-valued adic valuation attached to the height-one prime of $v$. Then for every Kähler differential $\omega \in \Omega_{F/K}$ there exists $g \in F$ with $\omega = g \cdot D_{K,F}(t)$, i.e. $\omega = g\,dt$. Only existence of $g$ is asserted; uniqueness is not part of the conclusion.
--
--   This is the existence half of the classical fact that, for a function field in one variable in characteristic zero, $\Omega_{F/K}$ is a one-dimensional $F$-vector space spanned by $dt$ for any non-constant $t$ — here non-constancy is supplied by $t$ having non-zero order at a place. It underlies the definition of the coefficient of a differential against $dt$ and of the order of a differential, and is used in the comparison of regular differentials under constant field extension.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_Place_exists_eq_smul_D_of_ord_ne_zero.lean

